$$SYNTAX-LEVEL 2
*(mgdb-gb-survey
<main class="mgdb-page mgdb-hub-page mgdb-gbsurvey-page" id="gbs-top">

  <nav class="mgdb-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span><a href="/genome">Genomes</a><span aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span><span aria-current="page">Genome browser survey</span>
  </nav>

  <section class="mgdb-hero-basic" aria-labelledby="gbs-title">
    <span class="mgdb-hero-basic-icon" aria-hidden="true">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 84 84" role="presentation" focusable="false">
        <rect x="12" y="20" width="60" height="44" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="5"/>
        <path d="M12 32h60" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="5"/>
        <path d="M22 44h14M40 44h9M53 44h9M22 54h22M48 54h14" fill="none" stroke="#2f5e2a" stroke-width="4" stroke-linecap="round"/>
      </svg>
    </span>
    <div>
      <h1 id="gbs-title">Genome browser survey, 2006</h1>
      <p>The maize community was asked which genome browsers it used and what it wanted from one. 99 cooperators answered, and their answers chose the software MaizeGDB went on to run.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="mgdb-message mgdb-message-info gbs-archive-note" role="status">
    <div><strong>Archive.</strong> This is the report of a survey conducted in 2006. It is kept as the record of how the community's input shaped the browser, not as current advice. MaizeGDB today runs both <a href="/jbrowse">JBrowse 2</a> and <a href="/gbrowse">GBrowse</a>.</div>
  </div>

  <nav class="mgdb-section-tabs" aria-label="Sections on this page">
    <a href="#gbs-why" class="is-current" aria-current="true">Why it was run</a>
    <a href="#gbs-method">How it was run</a>
    <a href="#gbs-results">What came back</a>
    <a href="#gbs-conclusion">Conclusion</a>
  </nav>

  <section id="gbs-why" aria-labelledby="gbs-why-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="gbs-why-title">Why it was run</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="gbs-prose">
      <p>Based on the 2006 MaizeGDB Working Group report and the Allerton report, it had become evident that the focus of MaizeGDB must shift to better accommodate a sequence-centric paradigm. The aims were to&#58;</p>
      <ol class="gbs-aims">
        <li>show how the data at MaizeGDB relate to the maize genome;</li>
        <li>relate MaizeGDB's sequence data to other sequence information as it became available;</li>
        <li>become the keeper of maize's official set of gene models, which ensures proper nomenclature;</li>
        <li>create a way to compare the various assemblies and annotations simultaneously.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The feasibility of implementing a genome browser was investigated and the available software evaluated. Each had strengths and weaknesses, so deciding which best suited maize geneticists needed careful analysis &mdash; and, in the team's words at the time, the next generation of genome browsers should not be specified by a secluded group of programmers. So they asked.</p>
      <p class="mgdb-hint">The Working Group reports are on the <a href="/working_group">Working Group page</a>. The Allerton report and the blank survey form were offered here as downloads; neither file is on the server any more.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="gbs-method" aria-labelledby="gbs-method-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="gbs-method-title">How it was run</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="gbs-prose">
      <p>The MaizeGDB team worked with the <a href="/working_group">MaizeGDB Working Group</a> and the <a href="/mgec">Maize Genetics Executive Committee</a> to prepare and circulate the survey.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mgdb-metric-grid gbs-metrics">
      <article class="mgdb-metric">
        <div class="mgdb-metric-top">
          <h3>Contacted</h3>
          <span class="mgdb-metric-badge">Cooperators</span>
        </div>
        <div class="mgdb-metric-stat"><strong class="mgdb-metric-value">1,241</strong></div>
        <p class="mgdb-metric-description">Cooperators the survey was sent to.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="mgdb-metric">
        <div class="mgdb-metric-top">
          <h3>Responded</h3>
          <span class="mgdb-metric-badge">Replies</span>
        </div>
        <div class="mgdb-metric-stat"><strong class="mgdb-metric-value">99</strong></div>
        <p class="mgdb-metric-description">Completed responses, an 8% return.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="mgdb-metric">
        <div class="mgdb-metric-top">
          <h3>Using maize data weekly</h3>
          <span class="mgdb-metric-badge">Habit</span>
        </div>
        <div class="mgdb-metric-stat"><strong class="mgdb-metric-value">92%</strong></div>
        <p class="mgdb-metric-description">Spent at least an hour a week on maize data online.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="mgdb-metric">
        <div class="mgdb-metric-top">
          <h3>Bad-browser comments</h3>
          <span class="mgdb-metric-badge">Free text</span>
        </div>
        <div class="mgdb-metric-stat"><strong class="mgdb-metric-value">29</strong></div>
        <p class="mgdb-metric-description">Comments naming a browser the writer disliked.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="gbs-results" aria-labelledby="gbs-results-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="gbs-results-title">What came back</h2></div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="mgdb-leaf-heading">Time spent accessing maize data online</h3>
    <div class="mgdb-table-scroll">
      <table class="mgdb-table gbs-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Each week</th><th scope="col" class="mgdb-numeric">Respondents</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Two to five hours</th><td class="mgdb-numeric">39%</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">One or two hours</th><td class="mgdb-numeric">37%</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">More than five hours</th><td class="mgdb-numeric">15%</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">None</th><td class="mgdb-numeric">8%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="mgdb-leaf-heading mgdb-spaced">Genome browsers used</h3>
    <div class="mgdb-table-scroll">
      <table class="mgdb-table gbs-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Site</th><th scope="col">Software</th><th scope="col" class="mgdb-numeric">Respondents</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">MaizeSequence.org</th><td>Ensembl</td><td class="mgdb-numeric">66%</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Gramene</th><td>Ensembl</td><td class="mgdb-numeric">66%</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">TAIR</th><td>GBrowse</td><td class="mgdb-numeric">54%</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">MAGI</th><td>GBrowse</td><td class="mgdb-numeric">54%</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">PlantGDB</th><td>xGDB</td><td class="mgdb-numeric">54%</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">NCBI Map Viewer</th><td>Not available outside NCBI</td><td class="mgdb-numeric">42%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mgdb-hint">75% used MaizeSequence.org or Gramene, or both. The three GBrowse and xGDB sites were used by 54% each, though not always by the same people.</p>

    <h3 class="mgdb-leaf-heading mgdb-spaced">Feature rankings</h3>
    <p class="gbs-lead">Respondents ranked seven features. A <b>lower</b> score means a higher priority, so the list reads best to least important.</p>
    <ol class="gbs-ranking">
      <li><span class="gbs-rank-name">Ease of use</span> <span class="gbs-rank-score">1.9</span></li>
      <li><span class="gbs-rank-name">Visuals</span> <span class="gbs-rank-score">2.6</span></li>
      <li><span class="gbs-rank-name">Speed</span> <span class="gbs-rank-score">3.2</span></li>
      <li><span class="gbs-rank-name">Cross-species comparison</span> <span class="gbs-rank-score">3.7</span></li>
      <li><span class="gbs-rank-name">Multiple gene selection</span> <span class="gbs-rank-score">4.1</span></li>
      <li><span class="gbs-rank-name">Computational versus experimental data</span> <span class="gbs-rank-score">4.1</span></li>
      <li><span class="gbs-rank-name">Ontologies</span> <span class="gbs-rank-score">5.1</span></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mgdb-hint">Respondents wanted an intuitive browser that let them find the data they needed quickly.</p>

    <h3 class="mgdb-leaf-heading mgdb-spaced">In their own words</h3>
    <div class="gbs-prose">
      <p><b>Desired features.</b> Reach specific data through intuitive tools; downloadable datasets in various formats; better cross-referencing between sites; current data; tools that are quick to learn and apply. In short, less hassle and effort in reaching maize data.</p>
      <p><b>Bad browser examples.</b> Of 29 such comments, 66% named MaizeSequence.org or Gramene, both Ensembl-based, usually citing slowness as the main and sometimes the only problem. That is not simply a function of popularity&#58; PlantGDB, TAIR and MAGI were each used by 54% of respondents, but PlantGDB was named once, MAGI three times, and TAIR not at all.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="mgdb-hint gbs-originals">The figures above were published here as three charts, which are kept as they were&#58; <a href="/images/chart1.png">time spent online</a>, <a href="/images/chart2.png">browsers used</a>, and <a href="/images/chart3.png">feature rankings</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="gbs-conclusion" aria-labelledby="gbs-conclusion-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="gbs-conclusion-title">Conclusion</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="gbs-prose">
      <p>On the survey's results, the team supported the use of <b>GBrowse</b>, for five reasons&#58;</p>
      <ol class="gbs-reasons">
        <li>The three most desired features were ease of use, visuals and speed. Cross-species comparison, where Ensembl excels, ranked only fourth, and GBrowse had such capabilities by then through Synbrowse and CMap.</li>
        <li>Cooperators wanted specific tools built into the browser for their own research &mdash; finding genes between two markers, for instance &mdash; so the browser needed to be flexible in code and open to community involvement. GBrowse is a community open-source project with a customizable plug-in architecture.</li>
        <li>Sites using GBrowse drew significantly fewer negative comments than sites on other mainstream platforms.</li>
        <li>MaizeSequence.org and Gramene already served maize sequence through Ensembl. Serving the same data through GBrowse would let researchers pick the browser that suited the question &mdash; Gramene for comparison across clades, GBrowse for maize-specific problems, with custom code contributed back upstream.</li>
        <li>Browsers can link to one another, so choosing different software from MaizeSequence.org and Gramene enriched the toolbox at minimal cost.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="gbs-outcome"><b>What happened.</b> GBrowse was adopted and is <a href="/gbrowse">still served</a>. <a href="/jbrowse">JBrowse 2</a> was added later and is now the browser most readers use, reached from any assembly or gene record.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>
)
